<%# EVERY RAW CANDIDATE, IN THE ORDER THE SEARCH RETURNED IT.

    THIS IS THE COLUMN THAT JUDGES THE SEARCHES, and the ordering is the whole reason it
    exists separately from the picks above. Those are sorted by OUR ranking, which is
    the thing under examination; these are in the provider's own order, which is the
    evidence you examine it against. Shown one way only, a bad search and a bad ranker
    are indistinguishable — the operator could not tell "the archive handed us twelve
    books" from "we sorted twelve books to the top".

    AND THERE ARE FOUR SEARCHES NOW, so a second question joins the first: not only "is
    the search any good" but "is THIS VARIANT any good". The breakdown below answers the
    second one and every tile carries the variant that found it.

    FLOOR ROWS ARE NOT HERE. Our mirrored headshot and any URL the operator typed are
    inputs we control, not things a search found, so crediting this column with them
    would flatter the search. They appear in the picks above, where they belong. %>
<section data-test="found-section" class="space-y-3">
  <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 flex-wrap">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-heading">
      What the searches returned (<%= @found.length %>)
    </h2>
    <%# THE ORDER IS NO LONGER "the order it returned them", because there are now four
        answers rather than one. Sorting on the provider's own rank across all four reads
        as each search's best hit, then each search's second, and saying so is the
        difference between a reader trusting the order and puzzling over it. %>
    <span class="text-2xs text-muted">best rank first, across every search</span>
  </div>

  <%# WHICH VARIANT EARNED ITS QUERY — the row the operator tunes the variant list from.
      Its own section rather than a line in the honesty panel, because it is a
      MEASUREMENT of this athlete's searches and not a standing caveat.

      `found` IS THE MARGINAL CONTRIBUTION, not the variant's whole answer: a photograph
      several variants returned is credited to the FIRST of them
      (Appearances::GatherReferencePhotos#harvest), so a variant's number here is what
      would be LOST by dropping it. That is the number a decision to drop it turns on,
      and it is why these columns do not add up to 80.

      A ZERO ROW IS THE POINT OF THE TABLE and is rendered as such. %>
  <% if @variant_breakdown.present? && @found.any? %>
    <div data-test="variant-breakdown" class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3">
      <p class="text-2xs text-muted mb-2">
        What each search contributed that the ones before it had not already found.
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-1">
        <% @variant_breakdown.each do |row| %>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 flex-wrap text-xs"
              data-test="variant-row"
              data-variant-found="<%= row[:found] %>"
              data-variant-chosen="<%= row[:chosen] %>">
            <span class="font-mono text-heading">
              <%= search_variant_label(row[:query], subject: @search_subject) %>
            </span>
            <span class="text-muted">
              <%= row[:found] %> found
              &middot;
              <%= row[:chosen] %> in the model
            </span>
            <% if row[:found].zero? %>
              <span class="badge text-3xs bg-warning/10 text-warning-ink border-warning/40">
                nothing this one alone found
              </span>
            <% end %>
          </li>
        <% end %>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% if @found.any? %>
    <%# THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER, as counts per verdict. On a real Commons answer for
        "Drew Lock" the proportion is the finding: 12 of 20 rows were scanned books.
        Twelve more tiles would bury that; one row of counts states it. %>
    <% breakdown = rejection_breakdown(@found) %>
    <% if breakdown.any? %>
      <div data-test="rejection-breakdown" class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
        <span class="badge text-3xs bg-success/10 text-success-ink border-success/40">
          <%= @found.count(&:chosen?) %> chosen
        </span>
        <% breakdown.each do |label, count| %>
          <span class="badge text-3xs bg-surface-alt text-muted border-subtle">
            <%= count %> <%= label %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <div data-test="found-gallery" class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-5 gap-2">
      <%= render partial: "appearances/reference_photo", collection: @found, as: :photo,
                 locals: { calibration: true, person_name: @person.full_name,
                           subject: @search_subject } %>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <div data-test="no-candidates" class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-heading font-bold">No search has run yet.</p>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">
        Nothing has been searched for <%= @person.full_name %>, so there is nothing to
        judge. The photographs above, if any, are the ones we already had: our own
        mirrored headshot plus any URL typed on the look.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</section>
